# Expectation record for 02_constant_expressions/006_conditional_and_comma_folding.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 006_conditional_and_comma_folding
area               = 02_constant_expressions
description        = Folding through the conditional and comma operators, folded and runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
This program contains no undefined behaviour, which is the property the differential oracle
rests on.  The two operators under test are precisely the two that impose a sequence point,
so the ordering of every side effect printed here is fixed by the standard rather than left
unspecified: the comma operator sequences all side effects of its left operand before its
right operand is evaluated (C11 6.5.17p2), and the conditional operator evaluates exactly one
of its second and third operands after its controlling expression (C11 6.5.15p4).  Nothing
here depends on unspecified evaluation order, which requirement 1 forbids.
No object is modified twice between two sequence points.  Every modification of a counter or
an accumulator is separated from the next by a comma operator's sequence point or by a
statement boundary, and the program contains no expression of the form v = v++, no v++ + v++,
and no modification of an object outside a comma chain that the same chain also modifies.
Every comma expression's left operand performs a real side effect -- an assignment to
volatile or to plain storage, an increment, or a for-clause -- so no operand is evaluated for
nothing, and holding the observed counters in volatile storage is what makes the ordering part
of the program's observable behaviour instead of an unobservable internal detail.
No call receives more than one argument with a side effect.  Where a printed line needs both a
value produced by a side-effecting expression and a counter read afterwards, the side effect is
sequenced in a statement of its own first and the counter is snapshotted into a plain local
before the call, so nothing depends on the unspecified order in which arguments are evaluated.
Otherwise: no signed overflow -- every signed result is small except the long long product
6 * 1000000000, which is 6000000000 and inside the range C11 guarantees for long long; the one
unsigned result printed as 4294967295 is modular reduction, which C11 6.2.5p9 defines for every
value and which is therefore not undefined at all; no shift of any kind; no aliasing violation;
no read of uninitialized storage, since every object is initialized before its first read; no
pointer arithmetic and no pointer value printed; no dependence on padding bytes or on the
addresses of unrelated objects; and sizeof does not evaluate its operand (C11 6.5.3.4p2), so the
volatile objects that appear inside a sizeof are never accessed there.
This program names no header at all and declares printf by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.  Every value is printed with %d or %lld matched
exactly to its argument's type, so no output is locale-dependent, address-dependent or
plain-char-signedness-dependent.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Three lines print (int)sizeof applied to a conditional expression, which reports the width of
the common type its two arms select: int against int gives 4, int against long long gives 8,
and unsigned int against unsigned int gives 4.  Those widths are implementation-defined in
principle, because C11 fixes only minimum ranges for the integer types.  They were measured
rather than assumed: the program was executed on all four supported targets at -O0, -O1 and -O2
-- twelve configurations -- and every one printed the same 67 lines byte-identically, including
fold_result_size_int=4, fold_result_size_llong=8 and fold_result_size_uint=4 together with
their runtime twins.  Because the widths agree on all four targets and the measured values
agree, cross-backend comparison of this program is sound and oracle (b) remains enabled for it.
The two measured differences between the supported targets cannot reach this program by
construction.  Plain char is never used and no character value is printed, so the plain-char
signedness difference -- signed on x86_64 and i686, unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64 -- is
unreachable here.  Plain long is never declared and never printed, so the i686 difference in
sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *), 4 there against 8 on the other three, is unreachable as well;
every wide value uses long long and is printed with %lld.  No address or pointer value is
printed anywhere.
What would legitimately break that assumption, and how it must then be handled: a target on
which sizeof(int), sizeof(unsigned int) or sizeof(long long) differed would still be
conforming, so the resulting difference would be an implementation-defined divergence rather
than a compiler defect.  It must in that case be recorded as an expected divergence with this
record's target list narrowed and the reason stated here -- never left to surface as a finding
against a backend that did nothing wrong, and never resolved by dropping the conditional
operator's result-type coverage, which is part of what requirement 2 asks this program to cover.
END
